[brief] Literature converges on a pure Higgsino thermal relic mass near 1.0-1.1 TeV to saturate Omega h^2 = 0.12, with sub-TeV masses viable only with slepton coannihilation or bino/wino mixing. Direct detect
**Survey** — 30 tools on 3 servers, 5 read-only probes, 1 papers found.

Literature converges on a pure Higgsino thermal relic mass near 1.0-1.1 TeV to saturate Omega h^2 = 0.12, with sub-TeV masses viable only with slepton coannihilation or bino/wino mixing. Direct detection of pure Higgsino is loop-suppressed due to accidental cancellations, leading to purity constraints on gaugino mixing from LZ/Xenon. Recent 2026 preprints propose inelastic Higgsino scattering with delta ~ 350 keV as an interpretation of the LZ 248 keV nuclear-recoil event in 2.84 ton-yr exposure, while a contrasting solar-capture analysis using IceCube non-observation of high-energy neutrinos from the Sun derives delta > 566 keV for ~1.08 TeV Higgsino, excluding the thermal-Higgsino explanation of the LZ event under standard halo and solar model assumptions. The tension hinges on halo high-speed tail (LMC), solar density/capture on heavy elements, elastic cooling in the Sun, and the LZ high-energy sideband joint fit.

Quantities worth checking: Thermal relic density Omega h^2 vs m_chi (thermal target 1.1 TeV and sub-TeV with coannihilation); Inelastic mass splitting delta [keV] and kinematic threshold for xenon recoil at 248 keV; Spin-independent elastic loop cross section sigma_SI [cm^2] vs LZ 2024-2026 limit; Inelastic SI cross section and differential recoil rate dR/dE at 200-300 keV in xenon; Solar capture rate C [s^-1] and equilibrium annihilation rate Gamma_A into W+W- vs IceCube limit ~1.5e19 s^-1 at Earth; Combined likelihood: relic density + LZ + Fermi-LAT dwarf + Planck CMB vs m_chi-delta plane.
